    <h1>The history of the world’s<br>most lethal <em>hemorrhagic fevers</em></h1>
    <p class="hero-sub">Ebola and Marburg viruses share a family, a transmission pattern, and a half-century of spillovers from the forests of Central Africa. Neither has a broadly available treatment. Together they account for thousands of deaths across more than 60 recorded outbreaks — most of which the world outside the affected region never heard about.</p>
