  <!-- RESERVOIR BOX -->
  <section class="taxonomy-section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-hd">
        <div class="t disp">The confirmed reservoir and the candidate treatment pipeline</div>
        <span class="eyebrow">Genus Orthomarburgvirus · 2 known species</span>
      </div>
      <div class="taxon-grid" style="grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(260px,1fr))">
        <div class="taxon" style="border-top:2px solid var(--marburg-col)">
          <div class="sp">Orthomarburgvirus marburgense</div>
          <div class="abbr">MARV · Marburg virus</div>
          <div class="meta">Responsible for the overwhelming majority of outbreaks. The 2004–05 Angola outbreak remains the deadliest at 88% CFR and 374 cases. The 2024 Rwanda outbreak, with 23% CFR, showed that earlier treatment and better infection control can shift outcomes dramatically.</div>
          <div class="cfr-bar"><div class="cfr-fill" style="width:60%;background:var(--marburg-col)"></div></div>
          <div class="cfr-val">Typical CFR range 24–88%</div>
        </div>
        <div class="taxon" style="border-top:2px solid var(--faint)">
          <div class="sp">Orthomarburgvirus ravnense</div>
          <div class="abbr">RAVV · Ravn virus</div>
          <div class="meta">Originally isolated from a single case in Kenya in 1987 (the “Ravn” case), Ravn was reclassified as a distinct species in 2011. It was also identified in the 1998–2000 Durba, DRC outbreak. Phylogenetically distinct from MARV; its specific epidemiology remains incompletely characterized.</div>
          <div class="cfr-bar"><div class="cfr-fill" style="width:40%;background:var(--faint)"></div></div>
          <div class="cfr-val">Limited data · few confirmed events</div>
        </div>
        <div class="taxon" style="border-top:2px solid var(--contained)">
          <div class="sp">Reservoir host</div>
          <div class="abbr">Rousettus aegyptiacus</div>
          <div class="meta">The Egyptian fruit bat is the confirmed reservoir for Marburg virus — the only filovirus with a definitively identified natural host. Fruit bats show no illness. They roost in large colonies in caves and abandoned mines across sub-Saharan Africa and into the Middle East, creating predictable spillover risk at cave-entry sites.</div>
          <div class="cfr-bar" style="display:none"></div>
          <div class="cfr-val" style="color:var(--contained)">Confirmed · PCR and serology evidence</div>
        </div>
        <div class="taxon" style="border-top:2px solid var(--contained)">
          <div class="sp">Vaccine pipeline</div>
          <div class="abbr">rVSV-MARV · ChAd3-MARV · mRNA</div>
          <div class="meta">Unlike Ebola, no Marburg vaccine has cleared full approval. Several candidates using the same viral-vector platform as Ervebo (VSV-based) are in Phase I/II trials, with accelerated studies triggered by the 2023–24 outbreaks. WHO has listed Marburg as a priority pathogen for emergency-use assessment.</div>
          <div class="cfr-bar" style="display:none"></div>
          <div class="cfr-val" style="color:var(--watch)">No approved vaccine as of 2026</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

